<section class="tale-hero" id="top"><div class="tale-hero-copy"><nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/index.html">Home</a><span>/</span><a href="/stories.html">Stories</a><span>/</span><a href="/deities.html#vishnu">Vishnu</a></nav><p class="eyebrow">BHAGAVATA PURANA / SKANDHA 6 / CHAPTERS 1–3</p><h1>One name at the threshold.<br><em>A life given another direction.</em></h1><p class="tale-deck">Ajamila calls for his young son Narayana as death approaches. The answer does not erase his past—it gives him the clarity and time to transform what remains.</p><div class="tale-meta"><span>Bhagavata Purana · 6.1–3</span><span>About {{ storyCatalog.ajamila.minutes }} min read</span><a href="/sources.html#ajamila">View sources</a></div><a class="button" href="#disciplined">Begin the story</a></div><figure class="tale-hero-art"><img src="/assets/images/ajamila-hero.webp" alt="Elderly Ajamila reaching toward his young son Narayana while golden conch, discus and lotus light stops three dark cords at the doorway" width="1536" height="1024"><figcaption>A NAME OPENS THE THRESHOLD</figcaption></figure></section>
<div class="tale-introduction"><p>From the <strong>Srimad Bhagavata Purana</strong>.</p></div>
<section class="story-characters" aria-labelledby="characters-title"><p class="eyebrow">MEET THE CHARACTERS</p><h2 id="characters-title">Figures in this story</h2><dl><div><dt>Ajamila</dt><dd>A man once trained in learning and self-restraint whose choices estrange him from those values, and who later undertakes a changed life.</dd></div><div><dt>Narayana</dt><dd>Ajamila’s youngest son, loved deeply by his elderly father. His name is also a sacred name of Vishnu.</dd></div><div><dt>The Vishnudutas</dt><dd>Messengers of Vishnu who stop Ajamila’s removal and ask how justice should account for the divine name and a heart capable of change.</dd></div><div><dt>The Yamadutas</dt><dd>Messengers of Yama who arrive to carry out the consequences of Ajamila’s conduct and enter the story’s central debate.</dd></div><div><dt>Yama</dt><dd>Dharmaraja, lord of justice, who teaches his messengers that his own authority belongs within a greater sacred order.</dd></div></dl></section>
<div class="tale-layout"><aside class="tale-contents"><p class="eyebrow">IN THIS STORY</p><nav aria-label="Story chapters"><a href="#disciplined"><span>01</span>A disciplined beginning</a><a href="#diverted"><span>02</span>A life diverted</a><a href="#son"><span>03</span>The youngest son</a><a href="#threshold"><span>04</span>A name at the threshold</a><a href="#debate"><span>05</span>Justice and grace</a><a href="#awakening"><span>06</span>Grace becomes repentance</a><a href="#gangadvara"><span>07</span>A second life</a><a href="#yama"><span>08</span>The lesson reaches Yama</a><a href="#reading-note">Text &amp; interpretation</a><a href="/sources.html#ajamila">Sources &amp; reading notes</a></nav><a class="tale-back" href="/stories.html">Return to the collection</a></aside><article class="tale-body">{% include "reader-tools.njk" %}
<section class="tale-chapter" id="disciplined"><div class="tale-chapter-heading"><span>01</span><h2>A disciplined beginning</h2></div><p>Before the story names Ajamila’s failures, it remembers the life from which he fell. He is described as learned, truthful and restrained, attentive to sacred practice, guests, elders and his teacher. His conduct is not perfect by accident; it has been formed by study and repeated discipline.</p><p>One day his father sends him to gather fruit, flowers, firewood and kusha grass. Returning from the forest, Ajamila encounters a scene that overwhelms his restraint. He tries to steady his mind with what he has learned, but desire takes hold.</p></section>
<section class="tale-chapter" id="diverted"><div class="tale-chapter-heading"><span>02</span><h2>A life diverted</h2></div><p>The account does not describe a single private mistake. Ajamila abandons responsibilities to his wife and aging parents, forms a new household, and sustains it through dishonest and harmful means. The capacities that once served care and discipline are now turned toward appetite and survival without regard for those he injures.</p><p>Years pass. The Bhagavata’s moral force lies partly in that duration: one ungoverned impulse becomes a pattern, the pattern becomes a way of living, and the former life seems very far away.</p></section>
<section class="tale-chapter" id="son"><div class="tale-chapter-heading"><span>03</span><h2>The youngest son</h2></div><p>In old age, Ajamila is surrounded by a large family. The youngest of his ten sons is named Narayana. The child’s speech, play and ordinary movements become the centre of his father’s affection. Ajamila eats when the child eats and drinks when the child drinks; he delights in calling him near.</p><p>The boy is not responsible for the life that came before him. He is simply loved. Yet his name carries a meaning greater than anyone in the household appears to be considering: Narayana is a name of the Supreme Lord.</p></section>
<section class="tale-chapter" id="threshold"><div class="tale-chapter-heading"><span>04</span><h2>A name at the threshold</h2></div><p>Ajamila reaches his eighty-eighth year. As death approaches, he sees three fearsome messengers preparing to bind and carry him away. Terrified, he looks toward the child playing nearby and cries out the name he has spoken so often:</p><p><strong>“Narayana!”</strong></p><p>He calls his son. The sound is also the divine name. Before the messengers of Yama can proceed, radiant messengers of Vishnu arrive and command them to stop.</p></section>
<section class="tale-chapter" id="debate"><div class="tale-chapter-heading"><span>05</span><h2>Justice and grace</h2></div><p>The interruption becomes a debate. Yama’s messengers ask what authority could prevent the consequence of actions already committed. They describe dharma, the witnesses to conduct and the way actions shape future experience. They recount Ajamila’s earlier discipline and later harm precisely because justice cannot pretend that the past did not happen.</p><p>Vishnu’s messengers do not argue that wrongdoing is harmless. They ask whether punishment alone can cleanse the source from which wrongdoing grows. Ordinary expiation may address an act while leaving the habits of the heart unchanged. Remembrance of the Divine, they teach, reaches more deeply.</p><p>The name spoken at the threshold has opened a possibility that calculation did not predict. Ajamila is released from the cords. The messengers disappear, and the man who expected no more time is left listening to everything that has been said about his life.</p></section>
<section class="tale-chapter" id="awakening"><div class="tale-chapter-heading"><span>06</span><h2>Grace becomes repentance</h2></div><p>Ajamila does not wake from the encounter thinking that one word has made his former conduct admirable. He remembers the trust he broke, the wife he abandoned and the parents he failed to serve. His remorse is specific. He sees how uncontrolled desire carried his mind and how his actions caused suffering.</p><p>He also recognizes the gift inside the name he called without full intention. The unexpected rescue has not completed his transformation; it has made transformation possible. He resolves to govern mind and senses, to live without cruelty, to speak truthfully and to direct his awareness toward the Divine.</p></section>
<section class="tale-chapter" id="gangadvara"><div class="tale-chapter-heading"><span>07</span><h2>A second life at Gangadvara</h2></div><p>Ajamila leaves the household that had held his attachments and travels to Gangadvara, the gateway of the Ganga now associated with Haridwar. There he enters a disciplined life of contemplation. The change that began in a frightened cry is tested through time, practice and restraint.</p><figure class="tale-inline story-landscape"><img src="/assets/images/ajamila-gangadvara.webp" alt="Elderly Ajamila meditating with prayer beads beside the Ganga at dawn near the Himalayan foothills" width="1536" height="1024" loading="lazy"><figcaption>AJAMILA AT GANGADVARA</figcaption></figure><p>When death returns, it is not the first scene repeated. Ajamila sees the messengers of Vishnu again, bows to them and leaves the body with his mind established in the sacred presence he has chosen to remember.</p></section>
<section class="tale-chapter" id="yama"><div class="tale-chapter-heading"><span>08</span><h2>The lesson reaches Yama</h2></div><p>The story continues beyond Ajamila. Yama’s messengers return confused: if their order can be stopped, how many authorities govern the world? Yama answers that he is not the highest power. His work of justice belongs within an order grounded in Narayana.</p><p>He teaches them about devotion, the divine name and those whose lives have turned toward it. Ajamila’s rescue therefore becomes instruction even for the agents of consequence. Justice is real, but it is not the whole horizon. Grace can interrupt a trajectory—and when it is truly received, it produces responsibility, remembrance and a different life.</p></section>
<section class="tale-reflection" id="reflection"><p class="eyebrow">EDITORIAL REFLECTION / PAUSE &amp; REFLECT</p><h2>If mercy gives time,<br><em>what will you do with it?</em></h2><p>Ajamila’s second beginning is not a denial of consequence. It is the chance to face harm clearly and stop repeating it. Where might grace in your own life be asking for a concrete change rather than a feeling of relief?</p></section>
<section class="tale-reflection" id="reading-note"><p class="eyebrow">TEXT &amp; INTERPRETATION</p><h2>The name interrupts death.<br><em>The changed life interprets the gift.</em></h2><p>This page follows Bhagavata Purana 6.1–3. Ajamila calls the name intending his son, the Vishnudutas intervene, and he hears the debate. The source then explicitly narrates his remorse, resolve, departure for Gangadvara, sustained practice and later liberation. Leaving out that second life would change the episode’s shape.</p><p>The text presents profound claims about the power of the divine name. Traditions explain those claims with different theological vocabularies. This retelling does not turn the episode into permission to cause harm and rely on a last-minute word; its own narrative joins grace to awakened responsibility.</p><p>The threshold and Gangadvara paintings are contemporary devotional interpretations. Faces, architecture, clothing and landscape are imagined rather than historical reconstructions. The dark cords and luminous emblems are symbolic visual language.</p><a href="/sources.html#ajamila">Read the supplied-edition references</a></section>
